# Line endings — deliberately narrow.
#
# There is no blanket `* text=auto` here on purpose. With core.autocrlf=true on
# Windows, 95 of this repository's 97 tracked files are checked out CRLF; a
# blanket rule would renormalise all of them and bury a targeted fix under a
# whole-repository diff, with real churn risk in fixtures and test expectations.
# Add a rule below only for a file whose behaviour demonstrably changes with its
# line endings, and say what breaks.
#
# .gitignore: git's ignore parser does not strip a trailing CR (verified against
# git 2.55.0.windows.3). A blank line checked out as "\r\n" is therefore read as
# an EMPTY pattern, and an empty pattern matches every path ending in "/". Every
# directory then reports as ignored — `git check-ignore -v zzz_random/` exits 0
# and blames a blank line — so deleting a real directory rule such as
# `powerbi_report/` is undetectable on Windows, and `git add` / `git status`
# misbehave for every Windows contributor. Ordinary files are unaffected; only
# directories. Keeping the file LF in the working tree removes the misparse.
# See scripts/check_evidence_sinks.py, which gates evidence destinations on it.
.gitignore text eol=lf
